1. EC2 Instance Requirements & Setup
--------------------------------------
1. Launch two EC2 instances with the following specifications:
-> OS: Ubuntu 24.04
-> Instance Type: t2.medium (Minimum requirement: 2 CPUs, 4 GB RAM)
-> Storage: 15 GB
-> Security Group: Open SSH (Port 22) and the necessary Kubernetes ports.

Ports to Open in Security Group:
--------------------------------
1. Control Plane (Master Node):
-> 6443 (Kubernetes API server)
-> 2379-2380 (etcd server client API)
-> 10250 (Kubelet API)
-> 10259 (kube-scheduler)
-> 10257 (kube-controller-manager)

2. Worker Node
-> 10250 (Kubelet API)
-> 10256 (kube-proxy)
-> 30000-32767 (NodePort Services - optional for basic setup)



2. Prepare the Nodes (Run on BOTH Master and Worker)
------------------------------------------------------
=> Connect to both instances via SSH and perform the following steps on both machines.

Update the system and change hostnames on Both Node:
: sudo apt update -y
: sudo su

# On Master Node only:
hostnamectl set-hostname master
exec bash

# On Worker Node only:
hostnamectl set-hostname worker
exec bash


=> Disable Swap on both Node:
: swapoff -a
: sed -i '/ swap / s/^\(.*\)$/#\1/g' /etc/fstab


=> Forward IPv4 and let iptables see bridged traffic on both Node:
: cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/modules-load.d/k8s.conf
overlay
br_netfilter
EOF

sudo modprobe overlay
sudo modprobe br_netfilter

cat <<EOF | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/k8s.conf
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-iptables  = 1
net.bridge.bridge-nf-call-ip6tables = 1
net.ipv4.ip_forward                 = 1
EOF

sudo sysctl --system



3. Install Container Runtime & Kubernetes Components (Run on BOTH Nodes)
=> Install Containerd:
: sudo apt-get update -y
sudo apt-get install -y containerd
sudo mkdir -p /etc/containerd
sudo containerd config default | sudo tee /etc/containerd/config.toml >/dev/null
sudo sed -i 's/SystemdCgroup = false/SystemdCgroup = true/g' /etc/containerd/config.toml
sudo systemctl restart containerd
sudo systemctl enable containerd


=> Install Kubernetes Components (kubeadm, kubelet, kubectl):(On BOTH Nodes)
: sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y apt-transport-https ca-certificates curl gpg
curl -fsSL https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.29/deb/Release.key | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg
echo 'deb [signed-by=/etc/apt/keyrings/kubernetes-apt-keyring.gpg] https://pkgs.k8s.io/core:/stable:/v1.29/deb/ /' | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/kubernetes.list
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install -y kubelet kubeadm kubectl
sudo apt-mark hold kubelet kubeadm kubectl


4. Initialize the Master Node (Run ONLY on Master)
=> Initialize the control plane using kubeadm init. You will need to replace <master-private-ip> with the private IP address of your master node.

: sudo kubeadm init --apiserver-advertise-address=<master-private-ip> --pod-network-cidr=192.168.0.0/16
                                                  change master pvt-ip

=> Make sure to copy the kubeadm join command with the token that is printed at the end of the initialization output. You will need it for the worker node.
=> Set up local kubeconfig: 
: mkdir -p $HOME/.kube
sudo cp -i /etc/kubernetes/admin.conf $HOME/.kube/config
sudo chown $(id -u):$(id -g) $HOME/.kube/config

=> Install Network Plugin (Calico):
: kubectl create -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/v3.27.0/manifests/tigera-operator.yaml
kubectl create -f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/projectcalico/calico/v3.27.0/manifests/custom-resources.yaml

=> Verify the master node and pods are running:
: kubectl get nodes
kubectl get pods -n kube-system



5. Join the Worker Node (Run ONLY on Worker)
=> Paste the kubeadm join command that you copied earlier from the master node initialization output into your worker node's terminal.
: sudo kubeadm join <master-private-ip>:6443 --token <token> --discovery-token-ca-cert-hash sha256:<hash>
                     pvt-ip

=> To verify the cluster is fully functional, go back to the Master Node and run:
: kubectl get nodes -o wide

=> Both the master and worker nodes should eventually display a Ready status.



Create the Nginx Deployment
===========================

=> Create a deployment named nginx-test using the official Nginx image. This tells Kubernetes to pull the image and start a pod on your worker node.
: kubectl create deployment nginx-test --image=nginx

=> Verify it is running:
: kubectl get pods
Note: Wait until the status changes from ContainerCreating to Running.

2. Expose the Deployment
=> To access the Nginx web server from outside the cluster, expose the deployment using a NodePort service. This maps a port on your AWS EC2 instance directly to the Nginx container.
: kubectl expose deployment nginx-test --type=NodePort --port=80

3. Find the Assigned Port
=> Kubernetes will randomly assign a port between 30000 and 32767. Run this command to find out which port was assigned:
: kubectl get service nginx-test

